<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="E-notes">
      <title>Module 8: Eigenvalues, Eigenvectors, and Diagonalization</title>

      <objectives>

        <p>After completing this module, you should be able to:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><em>E1</em> Determine eigenvalues and eigenvectors of a matrix.</li>
          <li><em>E2</em> Relate eigenvectors to solving <m>(A-\lambda I)x=0</m> via row-reduction.</li>
          <li><em>E3</em> Use the characteristic equation to compute eigenvalues.</li>
          <li><em>E4</em> Distinguish algebraic and geometric multiplicities.</li>
          <li><em>E5</em> Determine when a matrix is diagonalizable and compute a diagonalization.</li>
          <li><em>E6</em> Understand similarity and its effect on eigenvalues.</li>
          <li><em>E7</em> Handle complex eigenvalues/eigenvectors for real matrices.</li>
        </ul>
</objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
For more details on this material, see
<url href="https://ximera.osu.edu/oerlinalg/LinearAlgebra/XLAChapter_eigenvalues/main">Linear Algebra: An Interactive Introduction (Eigenvalues Chapter)</url> and.
</p>
        <p>
          Consider
          <me>
            A=\begin{bmatrix}2 &amp;0\\0 &amp;3\end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>

        <p>Recall:</p>

        <md>
          e_1=\begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          e_2=\begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}.
        </md>

        <p>Let us explore:</p>

        <me>
          A e_1
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}2 &amp;0\\0 &amp;3\end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix}1\\0\end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}2\\0\end{bmatrix}
          =
          2e_1
        </me>

        <me>
          A e_2
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}2 &amp;0\\0 &amp;3\end{bmatrix}
          \begin{bmatrix}0\\1\end{bmatrix}
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}0\\3\end{bmatrix}
          =
          3e_2
        </me>
       
      </introduction>
 <image width="30%">
        <shortdesc>Visualization of Eigenvectors</shortdesc>
<alttext>Visualization of Eigenvectors</alttext>
            <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}

  \draw[->, very thick, blue!50] (0,0) -- (2,0);
    \draw[->, thick, blue!60!black] (0,0) -- (1,0);
  \draw[->, very thick, green] (0,0) -- (0,3);
    \draw[->,  thick, green!60!black] (0,0) -- (0,1);


  \node[below] at (1,0) {$e_1$};
  \node[left] at (0,1) {$e_2$};


  \node[left] at (0,2.6) {$3e_2$};
