%-------------------------------------------------- % Middle: Upper triangular %-------------------------------------------------- 
\fill[teal!30] (5.6,2.2) -- (3.4,2.2) -- (5.6,0) -- cycle; 
\draw[very thick,teal!70!black] (3.4,0) rectangle (5.6,2.2); 
\draw[very thick,teal!70!black] (3.4,2.2) -- (5.6,0); 
\node[teal!70!black] at (4.05,0.65) {$0$}; 
\draw[->,thick,teal!70!black] (4.5,2.7) -- (4.5,2.25); 
%-------------------------------------------------- % Right: Diagonal matrix / eigenvalues %-------------------------------------------------- 
\draw[very thick,teal!70!black] (6.8,0) rectangle (9.0,2.2); 
\draw[very thick,teal!70!black] (6.8,1.9) -- (8.7,0); 
\draw[very thick,teal!70!black] (7.1,2.2) -- (9.0,0.3); 
\node at (7.15,1.95) {$\lambda_1$}; \node at (7.55,1.55) {$\lambda_2$}; 
\node at (7.95,1.15) {$\lambda_3$}; \node at (8.75,0.35) {$\lambda_n$}; 
\node at (8.45,1.55) {$0$}; \node at (7.45,0.65) {$0$}; \node at (8.35,0.75) {$\ddots$}; 
\draw[->,thick,teal!70!black] (7.9,2.7) -- (7.9,2.25); 
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

<example>
  <p>What are the eigenvalues of</p>

  <md>
    A=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    1  &amp; 0  &amp; 0\\
    2  &amp; 3  &amp; 0\\
    5  &amp; 4  &amp; 0
    \end{bmatrix}
    \qquad\text{and}\qquad
    B=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    10  &amp; 4  &amp; 0\\
    0  &amp; -1  &amp; 4\\
    0  &amp; 0  &amp; -1
    \end{bmatrix} \, ?
  </md>

  <p>
    By the theorem on eigenvalues of triangular matrices,
  </p>

  <me>
    \operatorname{Eig}(A)=\{1,\,3,\,0\},
    \qquad
    \operatorname{Eig}(B)=\{10,\,-1,\,-1\}.
  </me>
</example>

<remark>
  <p>
    Is <m>A</m> invertible? No, because <m>0</m> is on the diagonal.
    Therefore,
    <m>\det(A)=0</m>.
  </p>
</remark>

<remark>
  <p>
    The scalar <m>0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if
    <m>A</m> is not invertible.
  </p>

  <p>
    Equivalently, <m>0</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m> if and only if
    the homogeneous system
  </p>

  <me>
    Ax=0
  </me>

  <p>
    has a nontrivial solution.
  </p>

  <p>Indeed,</p>

  <me>
    0\text{ is an eigenvalue}
    \iff
    (A-0I)x=Ax=0
    \text{ has a nontrivial solution}
    \iff
    A\text{ is not invertible}.
  </me>
</remark>
<section xml:id="independent-eigenvectors">
  <title>Eigenvectors for Distinct Eigenvalues are Independent (E2)</title>

  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>v_1,\dots,v_r</m> are eigenvectors of <m>A</m>
        corresponding to distinct eigenvalues
        <m>\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_r</m>, then
        <m>\{v_1,\dots,v_r\}</m> is a linearly independent set.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      This is a key reason eigenvectors provide an efficient
      coordinate system when the corresponding eigenvalues are distinct.
    </p>
  </remark>
</section>

<subsection xml:id="difference-equations-application">
  <title>Difference Equations Application</title>

  <p>
    Consider the discrete-time dynamical system (difference equation)
  </p>

  <me>
    x_{k+1}=Ax_k,
    \qquad
    k=0,1,2,\dots
  </me>

  <p>
    If <m>x_0</m> can be written as an eigenvector expansion
  </p>

  <me>
    x_0=c_1v_1+\cdots+c_rv_r,
    \qquad
    \text{where }
    Av_i=\lambda_i v_i,
  </me>

  <p>
    then repeated substitution gives
  </p>

  <me>
    x_k=A^k x_0
    =
    c_1\lambda_1^k v_1
    +\cdots+
    c_r\lambda_r^k v_r.
  </me>

  <p>
    This provides an explicit description of the solution whenever
    enough eigenvectors are available to express the initial state
    <m>x_0</m>.
  </p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="characteristic-equation">
  <title>Characteristic Equation (E3)</title>

  <definition>
    <p>
      The <term>characteristic equation</term> of
      <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> is
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)=0.
    </me>

    <p>
      The polynomial
      <m>p_A(\lambda)=\det(A-\lambda I)</m>
      is called the <term>characteristic polynomial</term>.
    </p>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      A scalar <m>\lambda</m> is an eigenvalue of <m>A</m>
      if and only if it satisfies
      <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <p>For</p>

    <me>
      A=\begin{bmatrix}
      2 &amp;3\\
      5 &amp;4
      \end{bmatrix},
    </me>

    <p>the characteristic polynomial is</p>

    <me>
      p_A(\lambda)
      =
      \det\begin{bmatrix}
      2-\lambda  &amp; 3\\
      5  &amp; 4-\lambda
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      (2-\lambda)(4-\lambda)-15.
    </me>

    <p>
      Solve <m>p_A(\lambda)=0</m> to find the eigenvalues.
    </p>
  </example>

  <example>
    <p>Let</p>

    <me>
      B=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      3  &amp; 0  &amp; 4\\
      0  &amp; 1  &amp; 5\\
      0  &amp; 0  &amp; 3
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>Compute the characteristic polynomial:</p>

    <me>
      \det(B-\lambda I)
      =
      \begin{vmatrix}
      3-\lambda  &amp; 0  &amp; 4\\
      0  &amp; 1-\lambda  &amp; 5\\
      0  &amp; 0  &amp; 3-\lambda
      \end{vmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Since <m>B-\lambda I</m> is triangular,
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(B-\lambda I)
      =
      (3-\lambda)(1-\lambda)(3-\lambda)
      =
      (3-\lambda)^2(1-\lambda).
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore, the characteristic equation is
    </p>

    <me>
      (3-\lambda)^2(1-\lambda)=0.
    </me>

    <p>
      Thus the eigenvalues are
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda_{1,2}=3,
      \qquad
      \lambda_3=1.
    </me>

    <p>
      The eigenvalue <m>3</m> has algebraic multiplicity
      <m>2</m>, while the eigenvalue <m>1</m> has algebraic
      multiplicity <m>1</m>.
    </p>
  </example>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="algebraic-vs-geometric-multiplicity">
  <title>Algebraic vs. Geometric Multiplicity (E4)</title>

  <definition>
    <p>
      Let <m>\lambda</m> be an eigenvalue of
      <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m>.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        The <term>algebraic multiplicity</term> of
        <m>\lambda</m> is the number of times it appears as a root
        of the characteristic polynomial
        <m>p_A(\lambda)=\det(A-\lambda I)</m>.
      </li>

      <li>
        The <term>geometric multiplicity</term> of
        <m>\lambda</m> is the dimension of the eigenspace
        <me>
          E(\lambda,A)=\operatorname{Null}(A-\lambda I).
        </me>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      For every eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>,
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{geometric multiplicity}
      \le
      \text{algebraic multiplicity}.
    </me>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Geometric multiplicity counts how many linearly independent
      eigenvectors correspond to <m>\lambda</m>.
      Algebraic multiplicity counts how many times
      <m>\lambda</m> appears as a root of the characteristic
      polynomial.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      A matrix is diagonalizable precisely when, for every
      eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, the geometric multiplicity equals
      the algebraic multiplicity and the total number of linearly
      independent eigenvectors is <m>n</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example>
    <p>
      Suppose the characteristic polynomial of a
      <m>4\times 4</m> matrix is
    </p>

    <me>
      p_A(\lambda)=(\lambda-2)^3(\lambda-5).
    </me>

    <p>Then:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda=2</m> is
        <m>3</m>;
      </li>
      <li>
        the algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda=5</m> is
        <m>1</m>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      If <m>\dim E(2,A)=2</m> (instead of <m>3</m>), then
      <m>A</m> is not diagonalizable even though
      <m>\lambda=2</m> appears three times algebraically.
    </p>
  </example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="similarity">
  <title>Similarity (E6)</title>

  <definition>
    <p>
      Matrices <m>A,B\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> are
      <term>similar</term> if there exists an invertible matrix
      <m>P</m> such that
    </p>

    <me>
      A=PBP^{-1}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Equivalently,
    </p>

    <me>
      B=P^{-1}AP.
    </me>
  </definition>

  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are similar, then they have the
        same characteristic polynomial and hence the same
        eigenvalues (including multiplicities).
      </p>
    </statement>

    <proof>
      <me>
        \det(A-\lambda I)
        =
        \det(PBP^{-1}-\lambda I)
        =
        \det\!\left(P(B-\lambda I)P^{-1}\right)
      </me>

      <me>
        =
        \det(P)\,
        \det(B-\lambda I)\,
        \det(P^{-1})
        =
        \det(B-\lambda I).
      </me>

      <p>
        Therefore, the characteristic polynomials of
        <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are equal.
      </p>
    </proof>
  </theorem>

  <remark>
    <p>
      The converse is not true in general.
    </p>

    <md>
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2  &amp; 1\\
      0  &amp; 2
      \end{bmatrix}
      \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
      (\lambda-2)^2=0,
      \qquad\qquad
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2  &amp; 0\\
      0  &amp; 2
      \end{bmatrix}
      \qquad\Longrightarrow\qquad
      (\lambda-2)^2=0.
    </md>

    <p>
      Even though these matrices have the same characteristic
      polynomial, they are not similar.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Row equivalence does not preserve eigenvalues in general.
      Consequently, row reduction is not an eigenvalue-preserving
      operation.
    </p>
  </remark>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="complex-eigenvalues">
  <title>Complex Eigenvalues (E7)</title>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Complex Numbers (Brief Review)</title>

    <p>
      A complex number has the form <m>x+iy</m>, where
      <m>i^2=-1</m>. If <m>\lambda=a+bi</m>, then its complex
      conjugate is <m>\overline{\lambda}=a-bi</m>.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        If <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> and
        <m>\lambda\in\mathbb{C}</m> is an eigenvalue of
        <m>A</m>, then <m>\overline{\lambda}</m> is also an
        eigenvalue. Complex eigenvalues of real matrices occur
        in conjugate pairs.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <example>
    <p>For</p>

    <me>
      A=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      0 &amp;1\\
      -1 &amp;0
      \end{bmatrix},
    </me>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)
      =
      \det\begin{bmatrix}
      -\lambda &amp;1\\
      -1 &amp;-\lambda
      \end{bmatrix}
      =
      \lambda^2+1.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
      <m>\lambda^2+1=0</m>, so the eigenvalues are
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda=i,
      \qquad
      \lambda=-i.
    </me>
  </example>

</subsection>
<subsection xml:id="diagonalization">
  <title>Diagonalization (E5)</title>

  <definition>
    <p>
      A matrix <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> is
      <term>diagonalizable</term> if it is similar to a diagonal
      matrix. That is, there exist an invertible matrix <m>P</m>
      and a diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that
    </p>

    <me>
      A=PDP^{-1}.
    </me>
  </definition>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>Why It Matters: Powers of a Matrix</title>

    <p>
      If
    </p>

    <me>
      A=PDP^{-1},
    </me>

    <p>
      then
    </p>

    <me>
      A^k=PD^kP^{-1},
      \qquad
      k\in\mathbb{N},
    </me>

    <p>
      and <m>D^k</m> is easy to compute because one simply raises
      each diagonal entry to the <m>k</m>th power.
    </p>
  </subsubsection>

  <theorem>
    <title>Diagonalization Theorem</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A matrix <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> is diagonalizable
        if and only if <m>A</m> has <m>n</m> linearly independent
        eigenvectors.
      </p>

      <p>
        Moreover, if <m>A</m> is diagonalizable, then
      </p>

      <me>
        A=PDP^{-1},
      </me>

      <p>
        where the columns of <m>P</m> are eigenvectors of
        <m>A</m>, and the diagonal entries of <m>D</m> are the
        corresponding eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <p>
    The equation
  </p>

  <me>
    A=PDP^{-1}
  </me>

  <p>
    implies
  </p>

  <me>
    AP=PD.
  </me>

  <p>
    Writing the columns explicitly,
  </p>

  <me>
    A
    \begin{bmatrix}
    p_1  &amp; p_2  &amp; \cdots  &amp; p_n
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    PD
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    d_1p_1  &amp; d_2p_2  &amp; \cdots  &amp; d_np_n
    \end{bmatrix}.
  </me>

  <p>
    Therefore,
  </p>

  <me>
    Ap_1=d_1p_1,
    \qquad
    Ap_2=d_2p_2,
    \qquad
    \ldots,
    \qquad
    Ap_n=d_np_n.
  </me>

  <p>
    Thus each column <m>p_i</m> of <m>P</m> is an eigenvector of
    <m>A</m> corresponding to the eigenvalue <m>d_i</m>.
  </p>

  <subsubsection>
    <title>How to Diagonalize a Matrix</title>

    <algorithm>
      <p>
        To diagonalize <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m>:
      </p>

      <ol>
        <li>
          Find the eigenvalues by solving
          <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          For each eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, find a basis for
          <m>E(\lambda,A)=\operatorname{Null}(A-\lambda I)</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          Count the total number of linearly independent
          eigenvectors obtained. If this number is <m>n</m>, then
          <m>A</m> is diagonalizable; otherwise, it is not.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </algorithm>
  </subsubsection>
  </subsection>
  <subsection>
  <title>Multiplicity Conditions</title>

  <theorem>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>A\in\mathbb{R}^{n\times n}</m> have distinct
        eigenvalues <m>\lambda_1,\dots,\lambda_p</m>.
      </p>

      <ol label="a">
        <li>
          For each <m>k</m>,
          <m>\dim E(\lambda_k,A)</m> is less than or equal to the
          algebraic multiplicity of <m>\lambda_k</m>.
        </li>

        <li>
          <m>A</m> is diagonalizable if and only if
          <me>
            \dim E(\lambda_1,A)
            +\cdots+
            \dim E(\lambda_p,A)
            = n.
          </me>
        </li>

        <li>
          If <m>A</m> is diagonalizable and
          <m>B_k</m> is a basis for
          <m>E(\lambda_k,A)</m>, then
          <m>B_1\cup\cdots\cup B_p</m>
          is an eigenvector basis for
          <m>\mathbb{R}^n</m>.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </theorem>
</subsection>

<example>
  <title>Full Diagonalization of a Matrix</title>

  <p>Diagonalize the matrix</p>

  <me>
    A=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    4  &amp; 1\\
    2  &amp; 3
    \end{bmatrix}.
  </me>

  <task>
    <title>Step 1: Find Eigenvalues</title>

    <p>Compute the characteristic polynomial:</p>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)
      =
      \begin{vmatrix}
      4-\lambda  &amp; 1\\
      2  &amp; 3-\lambda
      \end{vmatrix}
      =
      (4-\lambda)(3-\lambda)-2.
    </me>

    <p>Expanding gives</p>

    <me>
      \lambda^2-7\lambda+10.
    </me>

    <p>Solve the characteristic equation:</p>

    <me>
      \lambda^2-7\lambda+10=0
      \iff
      (\lambda-5)(\lambda-2)=0.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda_1=5,
      \qquad
      \lambda_2=2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Each eigenvalue has algebraic multiplicity
      <m>1</m>.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task>
    <title>Step 2: Find Eigenvectors</title>

    <p><em>Eigenvalue</em> <m>\lambda=5</m>:</p>

    <me>
      A-5I=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      -1  &amp; 1\\
      2  &amp; -2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>Solve <m>(A-5I)x=0</m>:</p>

    <me>
      -x_1+x_2=0
      \qquad\Rightarrow\qquad
      x_2=x_1.
    </me>

    <p>Taking <m>x_1=1</m> gives</p>

    <me>
      v_1=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1\\
      1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p><em>Eigenvalue</em> <m>\lambda=2</m>:</p>

    <me>
      A-2I=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      2  &amp; 1\\
      2  &amp; 1
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>Solve <m>(A-2I)x=0</m>:</p>

    <me>
      2x_1+x_2=0
      \qquad\Rightarrow\qquad
      x_2=-2x_1.
    </me>

    <p>Taking <m>x_1=1</m> gives</p>

    <me>
      v_2=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1\\
      -2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </task>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Each eigenspace has dimension <m>1</m>, which matches its
      algebraic multiplicity. Therefore,
      <m>A</m> is diagonalizable.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <task>
    <title>Step 3: Form the Matrices P and D</title>

    <p>Place the eigenvectors as columns of <m>P</m>:</p>

    <me>
      P=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1  &amp; 1\\
      1  &amp; -2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Form the diagonal matrix using the corresponding
      eigenvalues:
    </p>

    <me>
      D=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      5  &amp; 0\\
      0  &amp; 2
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </task>

  <task>
    <title>Step 4: Final Diagonalization</title>

    <me>
      A=PDP^{-1}.
    </me>
  </task>

  <remark>
    <p>
      This representation makes powers of <m>A</m> easy to compute:
    </p>

    <me>
      A^k=PD^kP^{-1},
      \qquad
      D^k=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      5^k  &amp; 0\\
      0  &amp; 2^k
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>
  </remark>
</example>

<example>
  <title>A Non-Diagonalizable Matrix</title>

  <p>
    Show that the matrix
  </p>

  <me>
    A=
    \begin{bmatrix}
    4  &amp; 1\\
    0  &amp; 4
    \end{bmatrix}
  </me>

  <p>
    is not diagonalizable.
  </p>

  <task>
    <title>Step 1: Find Eigenvalues</title>

    <p>
      Compute the characteristic polynomial:
    </p>

    <me>
      \det(A-\lambda I)
      =
      \begin{vmatrix}
      4-\lambda  &amp; 1\\
      0  &amp; 4-\lambda
      \end{vmatrix}
      =
      (4-\lambda)^2.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore the only eigenvalue is
    </p>

    <me>
      \lambda=4,
    </me>

    <p>
      with algebraic multiplicity <m>2</m>.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task>
    <title>Step 2: Compute the Eigenspace</title>

    <p>
      Solve
    </p>

    <me>
      (A-4I)x=0,
      \qquad
      A-4I=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      0  &amp; 1\\
      0  &amp; 0
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Let
      <m>x=\begin{bmatrix}x_1\\x_2\end{bmatrix}</m>.
      The system becomes
    </p>

    <me>
      x_2=0.
    </me>

    <p>
      There is no condition on <m>x_1</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Thus
    </p>

    <me>
      E(4,A)
      =
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}
      x_1\\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}
      :
      x_1\in\mathbb{R}
      \right\}
      =
      \operatorname{span}
      \left\{
      \begin{bmatrix}
      1\\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}
      \right\}.
    </me>

    <p>
      Therefore,
    </p>

    <me>
      \dim E(4,A)=1.
    </me>
  </task>

  <task>
    <title>Step 3: Compare Multiplicities</title>

    <p>
      We have
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{Algebraic multiplicity}=2,
      \qquad
      \text{Geometric multiplicity}=1.
    </me>

    <p>
      Since
    <m>
      \textrm{dim} E(4,A)&lt; \text{algebraic multiplicity},
    </m>
      the matrix does not have enough linearly independent
      eigenvectors to be diagonalizable.
    </p>
  </task>

  <task>
    <title>Conclusion</title>

    <p>
      Because <m>A</m> does not have two linearly independent
      eigenvectors, it is not diagonalizable.
    </p>

    <p>
      In fact, <m>A</m> is already in Jordan form:
    </p>

    <me>
      A=
      \begin{bmatrix}
      4  &amp; 1\\
      0  &amp; 4
      \end{bmatrix}.
    </me>

    <p>
      This matrix is nearly diagonal, but the eigenspace has
      insufficient dimension for diagonalization.
    </p>
  </task>

  <remark>
    <p>
      This matrix is the standard <m>2\times2</m> Jordan block
      corresponding to the eigenvalue <m>4</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>
</example>

<summary>
  <title>Summary</title>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Eigenvalues <m>\lambda</m> satisfy
      <m>\det(A-\lambda I)=0</m>.
    </li>

    <li>
      Eigenvectors corresponding to <m>\lambda</m> are the
      nontrivial solutions of
      <m>(A-\lambda I)x=0</m>.
    </li>

    <li>
      Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are
      linearly independent.
    </li>

    <li>
      Algebraic multiplicity counts how many times an eigenvalue
      appears as a root of the characteristic polynomial, while
      geometric multiplicity is the dimension of the eigenspace.
    </li>

    <li>
      For real matrices, complex eigenvalues occur in conjugate
      pairs.
    </li>

    <li>
      A matrix is diagonalizable if and only if it has
      <m>n</m> linearly independent eigenvectors. If
      <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, then
      <m>A^k=PD^kP^{-1}</m>.
    </li>
  </ul>
</summary>
</section>
